    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Given points \\( A, B, C, \\) and \\( D \\) on a line in that order, with \\( AB = 2 \\) units, \\( BC = 5 \\) units, and \\( AD = 14 \\) units. We need to find the ratio of \\( AC \\) to \\( BD \\) and express it as a common fraction.\n\nFirst, we calculate the lengths of the segments \\( AC \\) and \\( BD \\).\n\n1. **Calculate \\( AC \\):**\n   \\[\n   AC = AB + BC = 2 + 5 = 7 \\text{ units}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate \\( BD \\):**\n   Since \\( B \\) is between \\( A \\) and \\( C \\), and \\( D \\) is beyond \\( C \\) on the line, \\( BD \\) is the sum of \\( BC \\) and \\( CD \\). We know \\( AB = 2 \\) units and \\( BC = 5 \\) units, so:\n   \\[\n   BD = BC + CD = 5 + CD\n   \\]\n   To find \\( CD \\), we use the total distance \\( AD \\):\n   \\[\n   AD = AB + BC + CD = 14\n   \\]\n   Substituting the known values of \\( AB \\) and \\( BC \\):\n   \\[\n   14 = 2 + 5 + CD\n   \\]\n   Simplify the equation:\n   \\[\n   14 = 7 + CD\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\( CD \\):\n   \\[\n   CD = 14 - 7 = 7 \\text{ units}\n   \\]\n   Therefore:\n   \\[\n   BD = 5 + 7 = 12 \\text{ units}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the ratio of \\( AC \\) to \\( BD \\):**\n   \\[\n   \\text{Ratio of } AC \\text{ to } BD = \\frac{AC}{BD} = \\frac{7}{12}\n   \\]\n\nThus, the ratio of \\( AC \\) to \\( BD \\) is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{7}{12}}\\).",
